In article <cafabb48.0206220035.74a0e225@posting.google.com>,
Brahim <bmo@xs4all.nl> wrote:
> We want that user to just pick up some files in his homedirectory
> /var/appl/logdir and not to be allowed snooping around on the system.

Run the ftpd in a chrooted environment:

First, in inetd.conf, replace "ftpd" with "chroot /var/jail/logdir /usr/sbin/ftpd".

ftp stream tcp nowait root /usr/sbin/chroot chroot /var/jail/logdir /usr/sbin/ftpd

Then in /var/jail, create a "mini unix" environment: you'll need to
duplicate /etc/passwd, /bin/ls, and of course /usr/sbin/ftpd.

You can now remove all the other users from passwd, and change his home
directory to "/logdir".

Now when ftpd runs all it can see is the contents of /var/jail.

You can do this with other applications, including telnet, if you want to
restrict access to specific files.

