On 14 Jun 2002 07:31:20 -0700, chris.mielke@drake.edu (Milkweed)
wrote:

>I recently installed ISC DHCP 3.0pl1 on a DEC 3000 running Digital
>Unix 4.0D. The install went fine, but when I try to start the DHCP
>server it gives an error stating "no bpf devices". I looked at the bpf
>and packetfilter man pages and apparently I need to compile
>packetfilter and the bpf extensions into my kernel. According to the
>man pages I need to add an "options PACKETFILTER" statement to my
>configuration file. However, I wasn't sure if there was something else
>I needed to do to compile support for the BPF extensions or if they
>were already integrated into packetfilter.
>
>Also, I'm new to Tru64 Unix so I've never compiled a kernel in this OS
>before. A quick outline of the steps I need to take to do the compile
>would also be greatly appreciated.
>
>Thanks in advance!

You need to do a 'MAKEDEV pfilt' in the /dev directory.  This is in
the packetfilter man page.Then, to compile a new kernel, edit it as
necessary then run 

doconfig -c NODENAME

where NODENAME is the name of you kernel config, found in
/usr/sys/conf.  So, just add the "options PACKETFILTER" line, compile
the new kernel and reboot with the new kernel.  That's it!

The kernel compile stuff is pretty well-documented in the man pages
and in the doc's.  I would highly recommend you read the docs before
you proceed.  You can get to the doc's online here:

http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com/docs/pub_page/V40D_DOCS.HTM

Also, there is a mailing list archive here:

http://www-archive.ornl.gov:8000/

This has a lot of questions answered, if I come across a tough one I
always go here first.

HTH

-- 
Trev

