Hi neil,

vmemory and data are the right parameters. Forget memory, by default
there is no real memory (RAM) usage limit - but you can enforce it by
anon_rss_enforce in sysconfigtab vm subsystem, see man sys_attrs_vm
for details.

the max vm space you can get in a process is controlled by two
parameters :

First one, virtual address space itself

# sysconfig -q proc | grep add
max_per_proc_address_space = 1179869184
per_proc_address_space = 4294967296

These are actual default and max values.

But generally, most of the vm space of a process is used by data
segment (bss consumed by malloc calls, and/or shared memory segment).
Therefore the second parameter involved is :

# sysconfig -q proc | grep data
per_proc_data_size = 134217728
max_per_proc_data_size = 1179869184

If you have a reasonably configured system in RAM, which is normally
the case in Oracle servers, set the default you need in the
sysconfigtab itself. If you are short on memory, set the limit using
ulimit -d xxx and ulimit -v xxx in the oracle user .profile file.

This will save you some wired memory (non pageable memory of the
kernel, where page table entry for processes are stored )


Hope this help,

Cheers,

