After you've backed up anything that was on the disks you're about to
redeploy and you've umounted them, you can delete the no-longer-needed
files in /dev:

cd /dev
rm rrb25[a-h] rzb25[a-h]
rm rrc25[a-h] rzc25[a-h]
rm rrd25[a-h] rzd25[a-h]

Create your raidset and assign a unit to it.  NOTE: the disks that you
said you want to use for the raidset (DISK230, DISK240, DISK250) are
all accessed through the same HSZ port.  You can still create the
raidset but you'll be cautioned that it has more than one member on a
port and that performance may suffer. It'd be much better if you could
physically relocate these disks on the shelves so that your raidset
was using, say, DISK130, DISK240, and DISK350.  If you have open drive
bays on other shelves, it'd be a good idea to move two of the disks to
other shelves.  The HSZ manuals explain how to move a disk.

Create the entry in /dev for the new unit.  Assume you assigned unit
201 (target 2, LUN 1) for the new raidset on the HSZ.  You'd need to:

cd /dev
./MAKEDEV rzb26
disklabel -rw rzb26 hszNN   (NN=40, 50, 70, etc.)

(The `b26' is called the logical device number and consists of a
letter (or not) and a number.  The letter portion comes from the LUN:
[0-7] ==> [a-h]  (though `a' is never specified).  The number portion
can be derived from the bus number and target using:

(bus_number * 8) + SCSI target

Then you can create your filesystem or file domain. For example, if
you want to go with AdvFS:

mkfdmn /dev/rzb26c some-domain
mkfset raidset1 some-fileset
mount -t advfs some-domain#some-fileset /mnt

Then you can restore the old data onto the new raidset.

Hope this helps...


